{
  "id": "Compound::DB00672 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpropamide, a sulfonylurea compound, is implicated in the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through its classification as an antidiabetic agent. The evidence suggests that Chlorpropamide, along with other sulfonylureas like Glipizide and Glyburide, is included in the broader category of Sulfonylurea Compounds, which are known to interact with pancreatic beta-cells to stimulate insulin secretion. This mechanism is consistent across multiple paths, highlighting a recurring role of sulfonylureas in modulating glucose homeostasis. The lowest common ancestors of Chlorpropamide and its counterparts—such as Tolbutamide and Glyburide—point to a shared classification within the Sulfonylurea Antidiabetic Agent category, reinforcing the collective antidiabetic function of these compounds. The integrated evidence underscores a central mechanism of action involving the regulation of insulin release, which is a key pathway in the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus.",
